  "name": "Recognizing governments that came to power by force — Latin America",
  "authorLine": "Agent run r6, 2026-10-25",
  "introductionText": "1. What FRUS answers directly. FRUS answers both halves of the question, and the answer changes several times. From the 1870s the Department applied a working test — is the new government in control, does the population acquiesce, will it honour treaties and claims — and it used the waiting period to collect specific promises: in 1877 it held off General Díaz until his government's \"disposition to comply with the rules of international comity and the obligations of treaties\" was shown (Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1877, Document 222, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1877/d222), and in 1903 the legation in Santo Domingo named four claims and concessions the provisional government had to accept first (Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1904, Document 228, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1904/d228). Wilson added a test of origin — constitutional succession and a free election, not force — announced in March 1913 (Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1913, Document 7, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1913/d7) and applied to Huerta and to Tinoco in Costa Rica. In Mexico in 1920–23 recognition was traded for guarantees of American property. From 1923 to 1934 the Department bound itself, for Central America only, to the 1923 treaty's rule that no government born of a coup or revolution would be recognized, then abandoned that rule in 1936 (Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1936, The American Republics, Volume V, Document 112, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1936v05/d112). In the war years recognition became a matter of consultation with the other American republics, focused on Axis influence; the Bogotá resolution of 1948 then tried to make continued relations the norm and to strip recognition of any judgment. After 1950 the documents show the Department asking, case by case, for elections, civil liberties, and — in the 1950s — assurances on communism and private capital. This rests on 43 documents, read whole, out of a scope of about 2,450 documents in 54 volumes (see the memo).\n\n2. Where FRUS points for the rest. Where the printed documents came from: more than nine in ten of the source citations in the scope are numbers in the State Department's Central Decimal File (Record Group 59; the subject-numbered filing system used from 1910 to 1963), overwhelmingly each country's \"political affairs\" file (8xx.00) and its \"government\" file (8xx.01) — Mexico's political file alone supplies more than six hundred documents; the bundled data names the decimal file only by its NARA identifier 302021 and carries no entry number, dates or access status for it, and none of these files is among the digitized ranges. Before 1906 the documents carry no file citation; the correspondence of that era sits in the Department's Diplomatic Despatches (NARA series 603720) and Diplomatic Instructions (593313) and the Notes from and to Foreign Missions (594363, 597272); the 1907 Honduras correspondence sits in the Numerical File (RG 59, microfilm M862, series 654171; case 7357 begins on roll 19794147). After 1963 the documents come from the Subject-Numeric Central Files (RG 59; POL 16 is the Department's own heading \"Independence. Recognition.\" in its 1963 handbook, and POL 23-9 \"Rebellion. Coups.\" from 1964) and the Central Foreign Policy File (RG 59, from 1973); from office files — Records Relating to Activities with the National Security Council (RG 59, Lot 62 D 1, entry A1 1583E–F, NARA 2838992, 1948–1961, partly restricted), the Executive Secretariat's Subject and Special Files (Lot 62 D 430, A1 1586C, 2839191, 1953–1961, partly restricted), Records Related to the Secretary's Staff Meetings (Lot 63 D 75, A1 1609, 2521274, 1952–1961, possibly restricted), the Secretary's and Under Secretary's Memorandums of Conversation (Lot 64 D 199, A1 1566, 602231, 1953–1964, unrestricted), Presidential Memorandums of Conversation (Lot 66 D 149, A1 5158, 610077, 1956–1964, unrestricted), the Bureau of Inter-American Affairs' Subject and Country Files (Lot 66 D 65, A1 5763, 1074073, 1962–1975, partly restricted), and Records Relating to Honduras (Lot 67 D 46, A1 5732, 2602882, 1957–1974, partly restricted); and from the presidential libraries' national security files — Johnson (National Security Files, NARA 567979; Latin America Country Files 567985, 1963–1969), Nixon (National Security Files, 572235; Institutional Files 572247) and Carter (Office of the National Security Advisor, 560131; North/South 560184); the bundled catalogue gives no access status for library series. What the editors cited without printing: far thinner — fewer than one document in twenty carries such a citation — and led by Nicaragua's political-affairs file (8xx.00 for 1910–1949), then Bolivia's, El Salvador's government file and Haiti's 1950s political file (all RG 59 Central Decimal File); the Rubottom files of the 1950s, which resolve to the Subject Files of the Office of the Assistant Secretary of State for Inter-American Affairs (RG 59, Lots 59 D 573 and 61 D 279, A1 1135, NARA 2108779, 1957–1959, unrestricted); Dean Rusk's telephone calls (Lot 72 D 192, which NARA divides among six series; the one the citation names is Transcripts of Telephone Calls, A1 5379, NARA 631706, 1961–1969); the Office of Andean Affairs' Subject Files Relating to Ecuador (Lot 70 D 247, A1 5741, NARA 26309336, 1964–1978, partly restricted); the Papers of George W. Ball at the Johnson Library (NARA 35300981); and Brzezinski's evening reports at the Carter Library (NARA 560156). The two channels are listed separately and are not added together.\n\n3. What this request cannot get from FRUS. (a) A complete census of decisions. I searched the editors' tables of contents in all 553 volumes for chapters naming recognition: the Latin American ones run from 1895 to 1950 and stop there, and before 1895 no chapter title names recognition of a Latin American government. A search for \"recognize/recognition\" near \"government\" with \"de facto\", \"revolution\" or \"provisional\" finds about six hundred unchaptered candidates in 58 volumes before 1895 (I read two, on Díaz), and a search pairing \"recognize/recognition\" with \"junta\", \"coup\", \"new government\", \"de facto\", \"provisional government\", \"military government\" or \"military regime\" across the 17 Latin American volumes of 1951–1988 finds about 110 documents in 15 of them; the phrase \"Department of State\" (present in 552 of the 553 volumes) and an impossible string (found nowhere) were run as controls. The full run of decisions after 1950 is on the shelf in the Subject-Numeric files under POL 16 (1963–1973) and in the Central Foreign Policy File, not in FRUS. (b) The Department's legal reasoning. A search for \"Legal Adviser\" within thirty words of \"recognize/recognition\" in the 54 scope volumes finds four documents; the shelf that exists for the later period is the files of the Assistant Legal Adviser for Inter-American Affairs (RG 59, Lot 75 D 163, A1 5773, NARA 12014843, 1961–1971, and Lot 81 D 324, P 871, NARA 17408805, 1968–1979, both partly restricted), whose contents I have not verified. (c) The Latin American side. Mexico's Estrada Doctrine appears only as reported to or discussed in Washington (28 documents in 18 volumes); the foreign ministries' own deliberations are not in FRUS and the bundled data offers no shelf for them. (d) The question's own words. \"By force\" turns up in the recognition chapters at about the rate it turns up anywhere in FRUS, so it cannot find this subject; the documents say \"de facto\", \"revolution\", \"coup d'état\" and \"junta\" (\"junta\" about eleven times as often as elsewhere).",
